---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Signalworks is one builder in Los Angeles making websites and web apps for service businesses nationwide. No team page, no stock photos — here's how that works in your favor."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
      <h1>One person. That's the pitch.</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top: var(--space-4);">
        Signalworks is a one-person shop in Los Angeles building websites and
        web apps for service businesses across the country. There's no team
        page because there's no team. You'll see "we" around this site — it's
        one person plus very good tools, and "we" just reads better on a
        checklist.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container container--narrow stack">
      <h2>The person who reads your intake builds your site.</h2>
      <p>
        Every part of your site comes from the same set of hands — the intake
        review, the copy, the design, the code, the launch, and the email
        answering your revision notes. Nothing gets lost between steps,
        because there are no steps between people.
      </p>

      <h2>Current tools, stated plainly.</h2>
      <p>
        I build with AI-accelerated tooling. It's why one person can deliver
        what used to take a team, in days instead of months, at a price
        without agency overhead baked in. I'd rather tell you that in plain
        terms than let you assume there's a floor of employees. The tools
        draft fast; the judgment about your business, your customers, and
        what goes on the page is mine, and I stand behind every word that
        ships.
      </p>

      <h2>Service businesses, specifically.</h2>
      <p>
        I build for businesses where the website's job is measured in phone
        calls — home services first: HVAC, plumbing, electrical, roofing,
        landscaping. But the focus is a specialty, not a fence: law firms,
        online stores, restaurants, and any business that lives on customers
        finding it are just as welcome. That focus is the product. The layouts,
        the copy style, and the checklists all exist because they serve how
        real customers choose a service business: quickly, on a phone,
        comparing you to two other tabs.
      </p>

      <h2>Los Angeles is where I sit, not where I stop.</h2>
      <p>
        Everything is delivered remotely — recorded video audits, video
        calls, and review links that work the same whether you're in Long
        Beach or Louisville. The city is home base. The work goes anywhere.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--rule">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Principles</span>
        <h2>The rules the work follows.</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Say what a thing costs and when it lands. In writing. First.</li>
        <li>The client owns everything. Anything else is a leash.</li>
        <li>Plain English or it doesn't ship — in copy, quotes, and invoices.</li>
        <li>No promises about rankings, traffic, or revenue. Deliverables and dates only.</li>
        <li>Speed is respect: fast pages for your customers, fast replies for you.</li>
        <li>If the honest answer is "you don't need me for this," that's the answer.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    heading="See if we fit."
    body="A few lines about your business gets you a straight answer — including 'you don't need me' when that's the truth."
  />
</Base>
